# noramedi-opscheck.env.example — F3-OBS-002
#
# This is a NAME-ONLY documentation template. It is safe to commit because
# every value below is either blank or a placeholder — NEVER fill in real
# values here or in any file that gets committed to this repository.
#
# On the production host, copy the variable names below into a real,
# NOT-git-tracked file at /etc/noramedi/opscheck.env, owned root:root, mode
# 0600, and fill in the real ping URLs there only. That file is what
# ops/systemd/noramedi-opscheck.service's `EnvironmentFile=` directive
# loads. Proposed host commands:
#
#   install -d -o root -g root -m 0750 /etc/noramedi   # install(1) does NOT create
#                                                      # parent dirs; without this the
#                                                      # next line fails on a fresh host
#   install -o root -g root -m 0600 /dev/null /etc/noramedi/opscheck.env
#   $EDITOR /etc/noramedi/opscheck.env   # paste real values, save
#
# No restart or daemon-reload is needed after editing this file. The service is
# Type=oneshot and systemd re-reads EnvironmentFile= on every ExecStart, so the
# next scheduled run (within 5 minutes) picks up the change. Restarting the timer
# would also reset its monotonic interval, which corrupts drill latency
# measurements — production-verified in both directions, F3-OBS-002 evidence §14/§18.2.
#
# Each of the five *_PING_URL variables is a secret operational credential
# (a dead-man's-switch ping URL from the external monitoring provider, e.g.
# Healthchecks.io-style). Anyone holding one can spoof a "healthy" signal
# and suppress a real alert for that check. Treat exactly like a database
# password or API token:
#   - never commit a real value to git
#   - never paste a real value into a docs/program evidence file, a PR
#     body, a screenshot, a ClickUp comment, or a Slack/Teams message
#   - evidence for F3-OBS-002 may record variable NAMES, check NAMES,
#     timestamps, and HTTP result codes — never the URL/UUID/key itself

# Ping URL for the pm2 check (noramedi-api + noramedi-worker PM2 status).
NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_PM2_PING_URL=

# Ping URL for the disk-usage check.
NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_DISK_PING_URL=

# Ping URL for the backup-freshness check (PostgreSQL dump staleness).
NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_BACKUP_PING_URL=

# Ping URL for the file-backup check (F4-FCR-001) — the off-host second copy
# of patient attachments, lab-order attachments and imaging images. A NEW
# dead-man's-switch check must be created on the monitoring provider for
# this; leaving the variable blank means the local check still runs and
# reports, but its result is never pinged (and the script sets the
# ping-failure bit 32 to say so).
NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_FILEBACKUP_PING_URL=

# Ping URL for the restore-rehearsal (drill) check (F4-FCR-001). Also a new
# provider-side check. Its grace period should be sized against the REHEARSAL
# cadence, not the 5-minute timer: with a weekly rehearsal the underlying
# local check only changes state once a week, so the usual ~15-minute grace
# still applies to the ping itself (the script pings every 5 minutes either
# way) — it is NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_DRILL_MAX_AGE_HOURS below, not the provider
# grace period, that decides how late a rehearsal may be before it alerts.
NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_DRILL_PING_URL=

# Ping URL for the PITR / WAL-archive check (F4-FCR-002). Only needed once the
# opt-in `pitr` check is switched on — see NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_CHECKS below.
# Create the provider-side check (e.g. `noramedi-pitr`) at the same time you
# enable it, not before: until WAL archiving is actually activated the check
# fails by design, and a provider check created early would just sit DOWN.
NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_PITR_PING_URL=

# Optional tuning — safe to set here, not secret. Defaults shown (scripts/noramedi-opscheck.sh).
# NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_DISK_THRESHOLD_PERCENT=90
# NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_BACKUP_MAX_AGE_HOURS=30

# F4-FCR-001 recovery status file. The filebackup and drill checks read this
# application-written JSON file instead of querying PostgreSQL: the monitor
# must keep working while the app is down and must not carry DB credentials.
# MUST match the application's own NORAMEDI_RECOVERY_STATUS_FILE (server
# .env) — if the two disagree, the checks read a file nobody writes and
# alert permanently. The systemd unit's ProtectSystem=strict makes the
# filesystem read-only to this service, which is fine: it only reads here.
# NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_RECOVERY_STATUS_FILE=/var/lib/noramedi/recovery-status.json

# Max age of the status file's own `generatedAt` stamp (positive integer
# hours) before the filebackup check fails. This is the "did the writer
# itself die?" guard — a status file that stopped being refreshed still
# contains its last healthy contents and must not read as healthy.
# NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_RECOVERY_STATUS_MAX_AGE_HOURS=30

# Max age of the last successful file-backup sweep (positive integer hours).
# Default 30 = a daily sweep plus 6h of margin, matching the DB-dump check.
# NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_FILEBACKUP_MAX_AGE_HOURS=30

# Whether a file-backup destination that is NOT in an independent failure
# domain fails the check. Default true (fail closed). With FILE_BACKUP_LOCAL_DIR
# on the documented single-disk production VPS, the "second copy" shares a disk
# with the primary and does not survive the disk loss it exists to protect
# against — so reporting it healthy would be the more dangerous default.
# Set to exactly "false" only to accept a same-host copy knowingly; the check
# then emits a WARNING line and passes.
# NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_FILEBACKUP_REQUIRE_OFFHOST=true

# Max age of the last passed restore rehearsal (positive integer hours).
# Default 192 = 8 days: one weekly rehearsal plus a day of margin, so a
# single late or skipped rehearsal does not immediately page.
# NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_DRILL_MAX_AGE_HOURS=192

# ── PITR / WAL archive (F4-FCR-002) ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# THE `pitr` CHECK IS OPT-IN AND OFF BY DEFAULT. Uncommenting the line below
# is how it is switched on, at the moment WAL archiving is actually activated.
# No unit-file edit and no redeploy is needed: the service is Type=oneshot and
# systemd re-reads this EnvironmentFile on every ExecStart, so the next run
# (within 5 minutes) picks it up.
#
# It is opt-in rather than defaulted-on because archive_mode cannot be enabled
# without a granted freeze exception AND a PostgreSQL restart. A defaulted-on
# pitr check would therefore be red on every five-minute run, indefinitely,
# for as long as that decision takes — and a check that is guaranteed red for
# weeks teaches the operator that red is the normal colour, which is exactly
# how a real alert gets ignored later.
#
# Listing the checks explicitly REPLACES the default set, so keep the other
# five when you add pitr.
# NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_CHECKS=pm2,disk,backup,filebackup,drill,pitr

# Where the PITR status document is published. Written by
# noramedi-pgbackrest-status.sh under its own systemd timer — NOT by the
# application, and NOT the same file as the recovery status file above. The
# opscheck never invokes pgbackrest itself; it only reads this.
# NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_PITR_STATUS_FILE=/var/lib/noramedi/pitr-status.json

# "Did the PITR writer itself die?" guard. Much tighter than the recovery
# status file's 30h because its writer runs every 15 minutes, and because a
# stale PITR document would hide precisely the fast-moving signal it carries.
# NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_PITR_STATUS_MAX_AGE_HOURS=2

# Max age of the newest ARCHIVED WAL segment. This is the assertion that
# catches a broken archive hiding behind a fresh backup: scheduled backups keep
# succeeding (each needs only the segments spanning its own start and stop)
# while inter-backup archiving is broken and the PITR chain has a hole. With
# archive_timeout=300 a healthy cluster archives at least every ~5 minutes, so
# 120m alerts long before the <= 60 min RPO target is genuinely at risk.
# NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_PITR_MAX_WAL_AGE_MINUTES=120

# Max age of the newest pgBackRest backup, and of the last `pgbackrest check`
# result. Both default to 30, matching the DB-dump check: a daily run plus 6h.
# NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_PITR_MAX_BACKUP_AGE_HOURS=30
# NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_PITR_CHECK_MAX_AGE_HOURS=30

# Whether a repository that is not in an independent failure domain fails the
# check. Default true (fail closed).
#
# During the stage-1 pilot the pgBackRest repository is LOCAL, so this will
# fail until an off-host repository exists and a restore has been proven from
# it. Setting it to exactly "false" accepts that knowingly: the check then
# emits a WARNING and passes. Understand what you are accepting — a local
# repository improves RPO and provides NO protection against loss of this
# host. Those are separate capabilities and must not be reported as one.
#
# Unlike NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_FILEBACKUP_REQUIRE_OFFHOST, this one is validated
# as a strict enum: any value that is not exactly "true" or "false" exits 64.
# A typo must not be able to silently disable a durability guard.
# NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_PITR_REQUIRE_OFFHOST=true

# Any malformed value above (non-integer, zero, or out of range) makes the
# script exit 64 — the config-error code — instead of running checks. That
# is deliberate: a silently-ignored bad value would let a check report
# healthy without ever comparing anything.

# Only ever set on the host temporarily, for the DRILL B controlled test
# (see the F3-OBS-002 evidence doc) — comma-separated check names
# (pm2,disk,backup,filebackup,drill,pitr) whose PING is skipped while the local
# check still runs and is still reported. Leave unset in normal operation.
# NORAMEDI_OPSCHECK_SUPPRESS_PING=

# ── EXIT-CODE NOTE (F4-FCR-002) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The pitr check was assigned bit 128. NOTHING MOVED: pm2=1, disk=2, backup=4,
# filebackup=8, drill=16, ping-transport=32 and config-error=64 all keep their
# meanings, so unlike the F4-FCR-001 revision no historical journal entry
# changes interpretation. Check bits can sum to at most 63, so 64 remains
# unreachable by any combination of check failures and still means, without
# ambiguity, "misconfigured, no check ran". A combined failure exit is now
# either 0-63 or 128-191.
